1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,970
Imagine you come from a country
where nobody picks wild mushrooms.

2
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:08,410
You're walking through a forest, and
you see someone carrying a basket.

3
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:12,050
They spend a few minutes
looking around under the trees.

4
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,550
Then they suddenly get excited.

5
00:00:15,457 --> 00:00:17,857
They pull a strange brown
thing out of the ground,

6
00:00:17,937 --> 00:00:20,627
brush the dirt off it, and
drop it into the basket.

7
00:00:21,717 --> 00:00:24,067
And you think: what on
earth are they doing?

8
00:00:24,637 --> 00:00:27,347
Then you find out they're
planning to eat it for dinner.

9
00:00:28,165 --> 00:00:30,165
Which seems stranger still
when you learn that the

10
00:00:30,245 --> 00:00:34,415
same forest holds hundreds of
different species. Some are delicious.

11
00:00:34,965 --> 00:00:36,735
Some will make you ill.

12
00:00:36,925 --> 00:00:38,715
And some can kill you.

13
00:00:39,358 --> 00:00:40,948
So why do people do this?

14
00:00:41,638 --> 00:00:43,158
Why is it completely normal in one

15
00:00:43,238 --> 00:00:45,768
country, and almost
unthinkable in another?

16
00:00:46,278 --> 00:00:48,878
And is mushroom hunting a
ridiculous thing to do...

17
00:00:48,958 --> 00:00:52,188
or a surprisingly sensible
way of getting food?

18
00:00:54,708 --> 00:00:58,338
Let's start with something that
sounds like a technicality, and isn't.

19
00:00:59,048 --> 00:01:00,458
A mushroom isn't a plant.

20
00:01:01,248 --> 00:01:02,858
It isn't an animal either.

21
00:01:03,588 --> 00:01:05,038
Fungi are their own kingdom.

22
00:01:05,828 --> 00:01:07,458
But if you had to pick a side...

23
00:01:08,209 --> 00:01:11,679
fungi are closer to us than they are
to the tree they're growing under.

24
00:01:12,469 --> 00:01:16,379
Animals and fungi went their separate
ways about a billion years ago.

25
00:01:17,189 --> 00:01:20,119
Plants had already gone
their own way, earlier still.

26
00:01:20,832 --> 00:01:23,382
So the thing in the basket
is a distant relative.

27
00:01:23,932 --> 00:01:25,802
A very distant one.

28
00:01:26,492 --> 00:01:29,662
And what you can see in the forest
isn't even the whole organism.

29
00:01:30,575 --> 00:01:33,605
The part with a cap and a stem
is just the fruiting body.

30
00:01:34,415 --> 00:01:37,355
Most of the organism is
underground, or inside wood

31
00:01:37,435 --> 00:01:40,345
— a network of threads
running through the soil.

32
00:01:41,105 --> 00:01:43,855
It's a bit like an apple.
The apple isn't the tree.

33
00:01:44,645 --> 00:01:46,255
And the mushroom isn't the fungus.

34
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:51,160
How much is hidden? In Oregon,
one single fungus spreads across

35
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:55,030
nearly ten square kilometres
of forest. One organism.

36
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:57,910
Thought to be thousands of years old.

37
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:02,170
By area, the largest living
thing anyone has ever found.

38
00:02:02,937 --> 00:02:06,527
And for most of the year you could walk
straight over it and see nothing at all.

39
00:02:07,397 --> 00:02:10,267
Then, for a few weeks, it puts
up something you can pick.

40
00:02:12,847 --> 00:02:16,297
Which brings us to the obvious
question. Why eat them?

41
00:02:16,407 --> 00:02:18,407
Because as food, mushrooms
are a strange choice.

42
00:02:18,487 --> 00:02:20,437
They're mostly water.

43
00:02:20,527 --> 00:02:22,017
They aren't a good source of calories.

44
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:26,843
So the honest answer isn't
nutrition. It's that they're there.

45
00:02:27,553 --> 00:02:31,653
Humans don't have to grow all their
food. Sometimes they can just... find it.

46
00:02:31,733 --> 00:02:34,043
Berries. Fruit. Nuts.
Roots. And mushrooms.

47
00:02:35,123 --> 00:02:37,073
You only had to know where to look.

48
00:02:37,863 --> 00:02:39,503
And here's the part that turns the forest

49
00:02:39,583 --> 00:02:41,853
into something more
than a free supermarket.

50
00:02:42,879 --> 00:02:45,529
Most of these mushrooms
cannot be farmed at all.

51
00:02:45,619 --> 00:02:48,009
Not because nobody has tried.

52
00:02:48,139 --> 00:02:50,129
Because of what they are.

53
00:02:50,809 --> 00:02:52,749
Underground, the threads
of the fungus wrap

54
00:02:52,829 --> 00:02:55,379
around the roots of a
tree and join with them.

55
00:02:56,289 --> 00:02:58,999
The tree hands over sugar
that it made from sunlight.

56
00:02:59,769 --> 00:03:02,449
The fungus hands back
water and minerals, drawn

57
00:03:02,529 --> 00:03:05,259
from far more soil than
roots could ever reach.

58
00:03:06,055 --> 00:03:09,665
It's a trade. It's called mycorrhiza,
and it is not a curiosity.

59
00:03:10,855 --> 00:03:14,005
The large majority of land
plants do some version of it.

60
00:03:14,695 --> 00:03:16,185
The forest runs on it.

61
00:03:16,886 --> 00:03:20,656
Which is lovely — and a serious problem
if you'd like to sell mushrooms.

62
00:03:21,986 --> 00:03:23,766
A fungus that has lived
off a tree for millions

63
00:03:23,846 --> 00:03:27,096
of years has lost the
ability to live on its own.

64
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:30,650
Give it a bag of sterile
sawdust and nothing happens.

65
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:33,010
It isn't looking for food.

66
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:34,930
It's looking for a partner.

67
00:03:35,793 --> 00:03:41,503
Now look at a supermarket shelf. Button
mushrooms. Oyster mushrooms. Shiitake.

68
00:03:41,933 --> 00:03:46,823
Every one of them is a species that feeds
on dead material and needs no tree at all.

69
00:03:47,333 --> 00:03:48,943
They can be grown in a shed.

70
00:03:49,739 --> 00:03:52,459
And the ones that aren't on
that shelf — the porcini,

71
00:03:52,539 --> 00:03:55,429
the chanterelles — are missing
for exactly one reason.

72
00:03:56,339 --> 00:03:58,269
Nobody can grow them in a shed.

73
00:03:59,031 --> 00:04:01,661
So the forest isn't a
cheaper version of the shop.

74
00:04:02,451 --> 00:04:05,681
For some of these mushrooms,
it is the only shop there is.

75
00:04:08,381 --> 00:04:13,551
Which would be a happy ending, except for
one detail. Some of them are poisonous.

76
00:04:14,501 --> 00:04:17,771
And the dangerous ones are not
dangerous in the way you'd expect.

77
00:04:18,679 --> 00:04:22,569
Take the death cap. Its toxin
doesn't attack your stomach.

78
00:04:23,199 --> 00:04:26,049
It jams the molecular machine
that reads your genes.

79
00:04:26,699 --> 00:04:28,749
Cells stop making proteins.

80
00:04:30,019 --> 00:04:33,889
The liver, which processes almost
everything you eat, takes the worst of it.

81
00:04:34,569 --> 00:04:37,089
But that isn't what
makes it deadly. This is.

82
00:04:37,169 --> 00:04:40,339
You feel completely fine.
For six hours. Often twelve.

83
00:04:41,471 --> 00:04:45,551
Then you're violently ill. And then
you get better. The symptoms fade.

84
00:04:45,631 --> 00:04:47,061
You appear to recover.

85
00:04:47,905 --> 00:04:51,275
Three to six days after the
meal, the liver starts to fail.

86
00:04:52,033 --> 00:04:55,213
That one species, on its own,
accounts for something like

87
00:04:55,293 --> 00:04:58,383
ninety per cent of fatal
mushroom poisonings worldwide.

88
00:04:59,213 --> 00:05:02,483
Not because it's the strongest
poison in the forest.

89
00:05:03,473 --> 00:05:05,073
Because by the time your body tells you

90
00:05:05,153 --> 00:05:08,043
anything, it has had
half a day's head start.

91
00:05:08,879 --> 00:05:12,209
So you might hope for some simple
rule. There are plenty of them.

92
00:05:12,939 --> 00:05:15,489
Poisonous ones turn a silver spoon black.

93
00:05:15,799 --> 00:05:18,189
Anything the animals eat is safe.

94
00:05:18,959 --> 00:05:20,569
Bright colours mean danger.

95
00:05:21,355 --> 00:05:25,265
None of them work. Silver
doesn't react to the toxin.

96
00:05:26,015 --> 00:05:29,005
Plenty of animals happily eat
things that would kill us.

97
00:05:29,175 --> 00:05:33,565
And some of the deadliest species
are a modest, unremarkable brown.

98
00:05:34,427 --> 00:05:36,257
Nature doesn't label anything.

99
00:05:37,007 --> 00:05:40,797
So mushroom hunting isn't: I see
a mushroom, I eat the mushroom.

100
00:05:43,387 --> 00:05:47,637
It's: I see a mushroom. I work out
which species it is. I am certain.

101
00:05:47,767 --> 00:05:48,967
Then I eat it.

102
00:05:49,521 --> 00:05:51,191
And that's the interesting part.

103
00:05:51,821 --> 00:05:54,591
Because a mushroom is not a
category you can work with.

104
00:05:55,341 --> 00:05:58,091
An experienced picker
doesn't recognise mushrooms.

105
00:05:58,361 --> 00:05:59,731
They recognise species.

106
00:06:00,799 --> 00:06:03,269
Dozens of them. Which
trees they grow under.

107
00:06:03,359 --> 00:06:05,309
Which month they appear.

108
00:06:05,459 --> 00:06:07,559
What the underside looks like.

109
00:06:07,639 --> 00:06:09,969
What the flesh does when you cut it.

110
00:06:10,672 --> 00:06:13,882
It's a biological database,
held in somebody's head.

111
00:06:14,312 --> 00:06:16,822
And traditionally, they
didn't learn it from a book.

112
00:06:17,412 --> 00:06:21,782
They learned it from a person.
Usually outdoors. Usually as a child.

113
00:06:24,312 --> 00:06:28,512
Someone points at the ground and says:
that one's good. That one you don't touch.

114
00:06:28,592 --> 00:06:29,842
And the child remembers.

115
00:06:31,129 --> 00:06:35,879
Thirty years later, they say the same
two sentences to somebody else's child.

116
00:06:36,609 --> 00:06:39,809
Do that for a few centuries and you
get a country where taking a basket

117
00:06:39,889 --> 00:06:44,679
into a forest and coming home with
dinner is completely unremarkable.

118
00:06:45,413 --> 00:06:47,698
In Czechia, a recent survey
found that seventy-three

119
00:06:47,778 --> 00:06:50,503
per cent of adults go
out and pick mushrooms.

120
00:06:50,813 --> 00:06:53,103
Not once in their lives.

121
00:06:53,253 --> 00:06:55,063
As a thing they do.

122
00:06:55,731 --> 00:06:58,561
Now take the same forest. The same trees.

123
00:06:58,831 --> 00:07:01,151
The same species, in the same places.

124
00:07:01,231 --> 00:07:02,941
And nobody picks anything.

125
00:07:03,331 --> 00:07:05,621
Not because it can't be eaten.

126
00:07:05,991 --> 00:07:08,201
Because nobody there sees it as food.

127
00:07:09,039 --> 00:07:11,269
Somebody noticed this on his honeymoon.

128
00:07:12,019 --> 00:07:14,819
In nineteen twenty-seven,
an American and his Russian

129
00:07:14,899 --> 00:07:17,609
wife were walking in the
woods in New York State.

130
00:07:18,438 --> 00:07:22,248
She saw mushrooms, ran off delighted,
and started filling her arms.

131
00:07:23,018 --> 00:07:28,348
He found the whole thing revolting. Same
forest. Same mushrooms. Same afternoon.

132
00:07:28,458 --> 00:07:30,048
Two completely different worlds.

133
00:07:30,952 --> 00:07:32,692
They spent the next thirty years trying to

134
00:07:32,772 --> 00:07:37,842
explain it, and gave the two attitudes
names. Mycophilia. Mycophobia.

135
00:07:38,592 --> 00:07:40,592
Love of mushrooms, and fear of them.

136
00:07:40,672 --> 00:07:42,942
Neither one is about the mushrooms.

137
00:07:45,942 --> 00:07:48,132
And that's a fascinating thing about food.

138
00:07:48,922 --> 00:07:51,152
What counts as normal
is mostly an agreement.

139
00:07:52,042 --> 00:07:55,832
Somewhere, snails are dinner.
Somewhere, frogs. Seaweed. Insects.

140
00:07:56,815 --> 00:07:58,755
Some people drink milk
from another species

141
00:07:58,835 --> 00:08:02,545
their entire lives and
never think about it once.

142
00:08:03,397 --> 00:08:04,847
Mushrooms are the same.

143
00:08:05,637 --> 00:08:08,747
One person looks at the forest
floor and starts planning supper.

144
00:08:09,517 --> 00:08:12,347
Another looks at the same thing
and thinks: that's a fungus.

145
00:08:14,987 --> 00:08:19,467
Which is a shame, because they can be
extraordinary. Fried in butter. In soup.

146
00:08:19,547 --> 00:08:21,387
In a sauce. In risotto. Pickled.

147
00:08:21,467 --> 00:08:22,917
Or with onion and eggs.

148
00:08:23,765 --> 00:08:27,575
And then there's drying, which sounds
like the dull option and is the best one.

149
00:08:28,185 --> 00:08:31,005
Because drying doesn't
only stop them going off.

150
00:08:31,085 --> 00:08:33,315
It changes what they are.

151
00:08:33,999 --> 00:08:37,079
Mushrooms are rich in the
compounds behind umami — the

152
00:08:37,159 --> 00:08:40,829
savoury taste you get from meat,
from broth, from aged cheese.

153
00:08:41,711 --> 00:08:44,431
As a mushroom dries, its
own cells break down and

154
00:08:44,511 --> 00:08:47,461
release a second compound
that does something strange.

155
00:08:48,411 --> 00:08:51,321
On its own, that compound
tastes of very little.

156
00:08:51,471 --> 00:08:56,321
But put the two together, and the
savoury hit isn't added. It's multiplied.

157
00:08:57,448 --> 00:09:01,208
In tests with the pure compounds,
mixing the two in equal amounts

158
00:09:01,288 --> 00:09:04,698
has been reported to raise savoury
intensity around thirtyfold.

159
00:09:06,077 --> 00:09:09,267
So a dried mushroom is not a
weaker version of a fresh one.

160
00:09:09,897 --> 00:09:12,907
It's a stronger ingredient
than the mushroom ever was.

161
00:09:13,557 --> 00:09:16,767
Which is why, in the middle of
winter, you can open a jar...

162
00:09:17,217 --> 00:09:19,367
and the whole kitchen
smells of the forest.

163
00:09:22,147 --> 00:09:25,707
And then there's the other
advantage. It's free. Well.

164
00:09:25,787 --> 00:09:29,557
Not counting the car. The fuel.
The boots. The basket. The knife.

165
00:09:30,447 --> 00:09:33,417
And several hours of your life
spent wandering about in the woods.

166
00:09:34,237 --> 00:09:36,827
But the mushrooms
themselves? Those are free.

167
00:09:37,577 --> 00:09:40,477
You leave home with an empty
basket, walk into a forest, and

168
00:09:40,557 --> 00:09:43,947
come back carrying several
kilograms of food that nobody grew.

169
00:09:46,437 --> 00:09:48,247
One thing, though, and it matters.

170
00:09:49,197 --> 00:09:53,307
Mushroom hunting isn't a competition to
see who brings home the biggest basket.

171
00:09:53,577 --> 00:09:58,407
And picking something you can't
confidently name is a genuinely bad idea.

172
00:09:59,257 --> 00:10:02,407
Some dangerous species look
a lot like edible ones.

173
00:10:03,221 --> 00:10:05,091
Getting a blueberry
wrong is disappointing.

174
00:10:06,361 --> 00:10:09,091
Getting a mushroom wrong is a
different category of mistake.

175
00:10:09,421 --> 00:10:12,691
Which is why the knowledge
still travels the way it does.

176
00:10:13,381 --> 00:10:17,371
From somebody who already has it,
standing next to you, holding the thing.

177
00:10:20,101 --> 00:10:23,191
So is mushroom hunting
ridiculous? Not really.

178
00:10:23,901 --> 00:10:26,041
It's the last ordinary
survivor of something

179
00:10:26,121 --> 00:10:28,431
humans did for almost all of our history.

180
00:10:28,561 --> 00:10:31,631
Finding food, instead of making it.

181
00:10:32,319 --> 00:10:35,329
And it survived because
industry couldn't take it over.

182
00:10:35,679 --> 00:10:37,749
Not for lack of money in it.

183
00:10:38,433 --> 00:10:40,583
The mushroom won't grow without its tree.

184
00:10:41,293 --> 00:10:43,163
And the knowledge won't fit on a label.

185
00:10:43,553 --> 00:10:47,103
So it stayed where it started.
In forests. In families.

186
00:10:47,233 --> 00:10:50,963
Passed from one person to another
by pointing at the ground.

187
00:10:54,163 --> 00:10:58,353
Today we can buy strawberries in winter,
and avocados from another continent.

188
00:10:58,903 --> 00:11:01,373
And mushrooms, in a plastic tray.

189
00:11:02,223 --> 00:11:05,013
But then you take a basket
and walk into a forest.

190
00:11:05,503 --> 00:11:07,813
And you start looking at
the world differently.

191
00:11:08,665 --> 00:11:12,815
You notice the trees. The moss.
The soil. Where the water sits.

192
00:11:13,585 --> 00:11:16,615
You start reading the ground for
places something might be hiding.

193
00:11:17,403 --> 00:11:21,773
Then you see it. A small brown
cap under the leaves. You pick it.

194
00:11:21,943 --> 00:11:25,813
And you realise that a few hours
from now, it might be on your plate.

195
00:11:27,943 --> 00:11:30,223
So the next time you see
someone disappearing into a

196
00:11:30,303 --> 00:11:34,033
forest with a basket, don't
assume they've lost their mind.

197
00:11:34,683 --> 00:11:36,703
They're collecting dinner
from a shop with no

198
00:11:36,783 --> 00:11:39,953
door, no shelves, and
nothing written on anything.

199
00:11:40,777 --> 00:11:43,947
And if they know what they're
doing, it makes a very good one.
